  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nLow level upslope flow could result in areas of fog Sunday\nmorning across extreme western Kansas. A dense fog advisory may\nbe needed if this pans out.\n\nThe current system bringing the light rain will move eastward\nSunday, leaving southwest Kansas in a cool and cloudy regime \nwith upslope flow. There is no strong front that would scour out\nthe low cloud. Thus, expect highs only in the lower to mid 40s \nfor most of southwest Kansas. Not much change in temperature is \nexpected tonight either with continued cloud cover along with \nlows in the upper 30s. A slow warming trend is forecast starting\nMonday; but the low cloud will be slow to clear due to the lack\nof downslope. With some sunny breaks, expect highs in the lower\nto mid 50s Monday, with upper 50s to near 60 for Tuesday. On \nWednesday a shortwave trough will pass to the north of Kansas, \npushing a downslope front through most of southwest Kansas. \nTemperatures in the mid 60s to near 70 can be expected from \nDodge City westward, with slightly cooler readings in central \nKansas. \n\nAfter the aforementioned surge of downslope, the winds will \nbecome southerly as an upper level trough forms over the western\nUSA, allowing low level moisture that had been suppressed into \nthe topics to return to the central high plains. Dewpoints as \nhigh as 50 may arrive by Thursday. However, lapse rates will be \npoor, which is typical of fall, so that instability should \nremain negligible, with moisture trapped beneath a mid level \ncapping inversion. There continues to be much uncertainty about\nthe speed of the next upper level storm system for late week; \nand recent ensemble means are depicting a faster and farther \nnorth solution. This is not a desirable outcome if one is \nlooking for widespread rains, especially since southwest Kansas \nwill be on the warm side of this system. There is also concern \nthat the associated cold front will pass before any mid level \ncooling and destabilization can occur. This time of year in the \nwarm sector it is helpful and sometimes critical to have mid \nlevel cold advection overspreading the moist/warm sector, which \nwould steepen lapse rates and allow for surface based \nconvection. If 500mb temperatures can drop to -16 or -17C by \nFriday as the upper trough passes, as opposed to -13 or -14C, \nthen we would stand a chance of experiencing a line of showers \nand t-storms. But for now a drier solution with little to no \nrain is preferred; and the best chance of pre-frontal rain is \nacross central Kansas per climatology. It takes a special \npattern to get large amounts of rain on the central high plains \nin November; and this is why it is climatologically so dry out \nhere in the cool season. Median November precipitation for \nGarden City and Dodge City are only .31\" and .42\" respectively, \nas opposed to 4\" across parts of the Deep South.",
